{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey municipal rent control authority","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law establishing or controlling rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances enforced by local boards. HUD-subsidized housing and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in municipalities that adopt a rent control or rent leveling ordinance; a tenant may ask the municipal rent board or clerk whether the unit is covered.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Units covered by a municipal rent control ordinance"},"exemptions":"Housing owned or subsidized by HUD, unsubsidized developments with HUD-insured mortgages that HUD finds have economic problems, and projects financed by the New Jersey Housing and Mortgage Finance Agency are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":"Municipal rent control ordinances govern covered units; HUD-subsidized and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to them.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years following completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":"Rents that are subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Exempts covered new buildings from municipal rent control ordinances for 30 years.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase procedure and unconscionability limit","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise the rent during a lease term; it must terminate the old lease and offer a new lease at the increased rent with proper written notice. An increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with municipal rent ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; before evicting for nonpayment of an increased rent the landlord must serve a valid notice to quit and notice of rent increase.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential tenancies"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(f)","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Condominium conversion rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion. Rents may still be raised to cover new services or amenities.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A residential landlord may recover possession only by the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process, and must have good cause. Each cause except nonpayment of rent must be described in detail in a written notice to quit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; no residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a written or oral lease without good cause. A judgment for possession must be entered before the tenant must move.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential dwelling units; written or oral leases"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer duties and withholding penalties","requirement":"A new owner must obtain the security deposit and accrued interest collected by the former owner and remains responsible for investing it, giving all notices, paying interest and returning the deposit with accrued earnings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies; duties attach when the property is sold or transferred, whether or not the deposit and interest are actually transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All residential rental units"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Willful withholding of a deposit of a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance: civil penalty of not less than $500 or more than $2,000 per offense, collected by summary proceedings and payable to the state entity that made the deposits. Unlawful use of deposit monies: disorderly persons offense, fine of not less than $200 or up to 30 days imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Credit report and rental application fees charged to applicants","requirement":"A landlord may charge a tenant for the cost of a credit report and may request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification. If an application is denied due to credit, the landlord must give the screening agency's contact information.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants of residential rental units; landlords may obtain credit reports and perform background checks through public records.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rental applicants"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"15 U.S.C.A. § 1681m","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":"The adverse-action notice requirement is described in the packet as based on the federal Fair Credit Reporting Act.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
